---
import Marketing from '../layouts/Marketing.astro'
import ContactForm from '../components/ContactForm.astro'
import { SITE } from '../data/site'
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content'

const posts = (await getCollection('blog', ({ data }) => !data.draft))
  .sort((a, b) => b.data.pubDate.valueOf() - a.data.pubDate.valueOf())
  .slice(0, 3)

const schema = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'Article',
    headline: 'The Process Is The Goal',
    description:
      'The Optio philosophy of education: autonomy, competence, and connection, rooted in Self-Determination Theory and the belief that learning matters today.',
    author: {
      '@type': 'Person',
      name: 'Dr. Tanner Bowman',
      jobTitle: 'Founder and Head of School, Optio Academy',
    },
    publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Optio', url: SITE.url },
    mainEntityOfPage: `${SITE.url}/philosophy`,
  },
]
---

<Marketing
  title="The Process Is The Goal | Optio Philosophy"
  description="The Optio philosophy of education: autonomy, real consequences, and credit for real work. Rooted in Self-Determination Theory and a decade in the classroom."
  ogImage="/images/og/philosophy.png"
  ogType="article"
  schema={schema}
>
  <!-- ========== HERO ========== -->
  <section class="bg-neutral-900 text-white">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 py-20 sm:py-28 text-center">
      <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-optio-pink uppercase tracking-wider mb-4">The Optio philosophy</p>
      <h1 class="text-4xl sm:text-6xl font-bold leading-tight mb-6">The Process<br />Is The Goal</h1>
      <p class="text-lg sm:text-xl text-white/70 font-medium leading-relaxed">
        Centuries of research have taught us what motivates humans to learn. Optio is a philosophy
        of education rooted in autonomy, consequences, and the belief that learning matters today.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ========== THE ESSAY ========== -->
  <article class="bg-white">
    <div class="max-w-2xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 py-16 sm:py-20 prose-optio">
      <p class="text-lg">
        Most schools treat learning as a means to an end. You study so you can pass the test. You
        pass the test so you can get the grade. You get the grade so you can graduate. You graduate
        so you can get into college. Everything points to some future payoff.
      </p>
      <p>
        We think that gets it backwards. The learning itself is the valuable part. The curiosity a
        student feels when they choose their own project. The persistence they develop when they
        push through something hard. The pride of creating something that matters to them. That
        isn't preparation for life. That <em>is</em> life.
      </p>
      <p>
        The diploma, the transcript, the college acceptance letter? Those are real, and they matter.
        But they're the byproduct of a student who learned how to drive their own education.
      </p>
      <p>
        Optio was built on a simple observation: students who get to make real decisions about their
        education are more motivated, more engaged, and more resilient than students who don't.
        Everything we do flows from that idea.
      </p>

      <h2>The missing piece</h2>
      <p>
        Traditional schools cover plenty of subjects, and most students can find something they're
        good at or something they care about. What's missing is simpler than you'd think:
        <strong>students rarely get to make meaningful choices about their own learning.</strong>
      </p>
      <p>
        I spent ten years as a full-time public high school teacher. I experimented with giving
        students unusual levels of autonomy in my classroom: letting them choose their own projects,
        set their own deadlines, even making attendance optional. Students who were labeled
        "unmotivated" by other teachers were thriving in my class.
      </p>
      <p>
        It wasn't about the subject or some special teaching trick. <strong>It was the
        autonomy.</strong> When students owned their learning, they showed up differently. My
        research backed up what I saw every day: intrinsic motivation grows when students get to
        make real decisions.
      </p>
      <p>Optio is what happens when you build a school around putting students in the driver's seat.</p>

      <h2>Three things every learner needs</h2>
      <p>
        Self-Determination Theory is one of the most well-researched frameworks in motivational
        psychology. It identifies three innate psychological needs that, when satisfied, produce
        intrinsic motivation.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Choice.</strong> Students pick their own projects, decide how to show what they've
        learned, and choose what evidence to share. There's no single right path through Optio. This
        is the piece that changes everything.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Competence.</strong> The work meets students where they are and pushes them just
        enough. When a challenge is too easy, students check out. When it's overwhelming, they shut
        down. The sweet spot in between is where real growth happens.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Connection.</strong> Students see themselves in what they're learning. The work
        connects to who they are, and family and mentors get to be part of it. Education stops being
        something that happens to you and becomes something you share.
      </p>
      <p>
        When all three elements are in balance, students motivate themselves. Traditional schools do
        a reasonable job with competence and connection, but they rarely give students meaningful
        choice over their own learning. Optio was built to restore that missing element.
      </p>

      <h2>Choice comes with consequences</h2>
      <p>
        Optio students decide what to study, how to approach it, what to create as evidence, and
        when to do the work. That freedom comes with something most schools avoid:
        <strong>real consequences.</strong>
      </p>
      <p>
        Students who put in the work earn their diploma. Students who don't, don't. We won't soften
        that or erase it. Consequences are how you know your choices actually mattered. If you're
        never experiencing consequences, you're never making real choices.
      </p>
      <p>
        Public school will give you a diploma for showing up. You'll earn one from us for doing
        something meaningful.
      </p>

      <h2>XP, not grades</h2>
      <p>
        Think about the question every student eventually asks in a traditional classroom:
        <strong>"What do I have to do to get an A?"</strong> That question tells you everything. The
        student isn't curious. They're calculating the minimum.
      </p>
      <p>
        With XP, there's no ceiling. Every piece of work earns experience. Every project adds to
        your total. The question changes from "is this good enough?" to <strong>"what else can I
        do?"</strong> Grades make students want to finish learning. XP makes students want to keep
        going.
      </p>
      <p>
        Letter grades penalize failure. XP recognizes failure as valuable learning. First draft
        submitted: +20 XP. Revised after feedback: +25 XP. Reflected on what changed: +15 XP.
      </p>

      <h2>The teacher rides in the passenger seat</h2>
      <p>
        Optio teachers look nothing like what you picture when you hear the word "teacher." Every
        student can be paired with one dedicated teacher, and their job isn't to deliver content or
        assign grades. It's to help the student take charge of their own learning and ride in the
        passenger seat while the student drives.
      </p>
      <p>
        When a student hits a wall, the teacher doesn't tear it down for them. Students tackle
        challenges on their own, and the teacher steps in when they're genuinely stuck. Support and
        accountability go together: an Optio teacher will celebrate a student's best work and be
        straight with them when they're coasting.
      </p>

      <h2>Built for every learner</h2>
      <p>
        Optio was designed from the ground up using Universal Design for Learning principles. The
        platform works equally well for students of all abilities, by default rather than as an
        accommodation.
      </p>
      <p>
        When you measure progress instead of test scores, and personalization instead of
        standardization, the whole concept of "learning disability" starts to shift. A student who
        struggles with timed tests might thrive when they can demonstrate what they know through a
        video, a project, or a conversation with their teacher. Optio doesn't ask students to fit
        into a system. The system fits around the student.
      </p>

      <h2>What this means for your family</h2>
      <p>
        The philosophy lives in a real school.
        <a href="/academy">Optio Academy</a> is a WASC-accredited online private school built on
        everything above, and your student's <a href="/academy#free-class">first class is free</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>

  <!-- ========== FROM THE BLOG ========== -->
  {posts.length > 0 && (
    <section class="bg-neutral-50 border-y border-neutral-100">
      <div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 py-16 sm:py-20">
        <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between mb-8">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-neutral-900">Go deeper</h2>
          <a href="/blog" class="text-optio-purple font-semibold hover:text-optio-pink transition-colors">All posts</a>
        </div>
        <div class="grid md:grid-cols-3 gap-5">
          {posts.map((post) => (
            <a href={`/blog/${post.id}/`} class="block rounded-2xl border border-neutral-100 bg-white shadow-sm p-6 hover:shadow-md hover:-translate-y-0.5 transition-all">
              <p class="text-xs text-neutral-500 font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider mb-2">
                {post.data.pubDate.toLocaleDateString('en-US', { year: 'numeric', month: 'long', day: 'numeric' })}
              </p>
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-neutral-900 mb-2">{post.data.title}</h3>
              <p class="text-sm text-neutral-500 font-medium leading-relaxed">{post.data.description}</p>
            </a>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  )}

  <!-- ========== CONTACT ========== -->
  <ContactForm
    source="philosophy"
    heading="Interested in learning more?"
    subheading="We'd love to hear from you."
    placeholder="What resonated with you? What questions do you have?"
    buttonLabel="Send"
  />
</Marketing>
